tasks.description:
Asked by the user in the editor-support review (2026-11-17), [editor-support.md](docs/editor-support.md) decision 5, as code actions:

- extract a component's definition into a file of its own, and import it;
- inline a `compmd` component's use as what it expands to;
- extract a selection into a new component;
- rename a component across the document and the files that import it;
- turn a leaf into a container, and back, where its slots allow.

Asked by the user 2026-11-09: a guide for Tauri hosts on the CompMD website, as **Part VI, Tauri apps**, of [the Developer's Guide](guides/developers/README.md) (decided with the user). It shares the book's examples, tests, PDF and search, and links to chapters 8 to 11 rather than repeating them. CompMD never refers to any application.

## Using the plugin
- installing `tauri-plugin-compmd`, `init()`, and the `Builder`'s `scheme` and `frames_dir`;
- the frames' `base`, which the plugin gives the page (`__COMPMD__.base`, so `createSandbox` needs none);
- building the frames with `buildHashedFrames` and `source`;
- the main window's CSP;
- upgrading the plugin and `@compmd/sandbox` together (one frame protocol).

## Packs
- Why a pack's module can't be given as a URL: the frames' policy allows scripts from their own folder alone (`compmd://localhost/frames/`).
- Serving it from the app's own asset scheme (such as `app://localhost/assets/<hash>/`), fetched by the page (`connect-src`), and given to the sandbox as code: `modules: (module) => ({ code: blob })`. The Mermaid pack is the worked example.
- The app's own trusted scripts and WebAssembly: one path-scoped `script-src` source and `'wasm-unsafe-eval'`, never `'self'` on the frames' scheme.

## Examples
From `examples/tauri`, in the guide's style: the code shown is code that's checked. A Tauri app can't run in the guides' browser tests, so its code is type-checked there, and `cargo make tauri-check` runs it.

## Appendix: what happens behind the scenes
- the custom scheme, and a sandboxed frame's opaque origin;
- WebKit's `'self'`, which matches nothing on a custom scheme, and `source`;
- content-hashed frame folders and `immutable` caching (WebKit caches custom-scheme responses);
- the headers the plugin writes;
- what keeps a frame's code from Tauri's commands: the invoke key, injected into the main frame only, not capabilities ([the plugin's design](docs/tauri-plugin.md), decision 8).

## Links and checks
Linked from chapters 11 (*The sandbox, closer*) and 18 (*Other hosts*), the plugin's README and the guides' index. The guides' tests and `cargo make site` pass.

`@compmd/sandbox/check`, [hosting](docs/hosting.md) decision 11: a page and a document every host runs in its real browser or webview, generalized from the Tauri example's `--check`.

## It checks
- a strict frame loads from the origin; in it the origin is opaque, `fetch` is refused, and there is no host API: the adapter names what to look for (`__TAURI_INTERNALS__`, Electron's `ipcRenderer`), and the check always looks for WebKit's `window.webkit.messageHandlers.ipc` ([the Tauri plugin](docs/tauri-plugin.md), Risks, layer 3);
- a code block is highlighted;
- a bundled pack's module, and a stored one, load by URL and draw;
- files arrive with exactly the contract's headers; a path outside it is `404`.

It reports passed or failed with what failed, for the adapter to turn into an exit code or a test.

## Tests
Its own browser tests (a passing host and one with each failure).

Decided with the user (2026-11-25), for CMD-410: one Vite project can hold pages whose documents are written by different people. The site's pages are the maintainers'; the Playground's are anyone's. Each needs its own pack settings: trust, ambient packs, registries, fetching, and origins. Today `compmd.config.json` belongs to the whole Vite project.

## Change

A design in `docs/` (decisions first; [docs/vite.md](docs/vite.md) is the plugin's current design), reviewed with the user before any code:

- which configuration a page gets: the nearest `compmd.config.json` to its HTML entry, or something else;
- how `virtual:compmd`, the frames, the packs and the libraries are built and served for each configuration, in the dev server and the build;
- what is shared between configurations and what never is, against spec §15 (trusted code, sandboxes per trust, packs the configuration doesn't list);
- the security cases: a shared Playground document meeting the site's trusted pack, every origin allowed for the site's own documents but not for others', registry access;
- how a project with one configuration keeps working unchanged;
- the examples it is tested on: the website with its Playground, and an app with an editorial page and a page of readers' documents.

Its open points are listed up front and settled with the user.

## Done when

The design is approved in its review, and the implementation task's description follows it.

Task 1 of [the language server's design](docs/language-server.md#tasks) (decisions 2, 3, 5 and 6; Q1 and Q2).

A crate of its own, `crates/project/` (`compmd-project`), beside `compmd-host`:

- the root of a document: the nearest `compmd.config.json` (or a `package.json` with a `compmd` object), else the workspace folder, else the document's folder (decision 2);
- the configuration, as `@compmd/vite`'s `readConfig` reads it, with the new key `editor.suggest` (Q2) in the schema and checked as a list of scopes;
- the `Packs` sources for folder packs and installed packs (found as `@compmd/vite` finds them), and the lock files' integrity;
- files read through a trait of its own (a file's text, a folder's entries): `std::fs` natively, functions passed in for WebAssembly. No file system of its own.

## Tests

Unit tests for roots and configurations; `@compmd/vite`'s fixtures run against the crate.

Task 10 of [the language server's design](docs/language-server.md#templates-code-actions-refactorings-in-1), the IN-1 ideas it holds:

- `quickfix`: a diagnostic's `fix`; importing a pack's component for an unknown name, from the project's packs;
- `source.organizeImports`;
- `source.organizeFrontmatter.compmd`, with the common keys ([Q7](docs/language-server.md#:~:text=Q7.)) offered one by one, never added unasked;
- the frontmatter from YAML to TOML and back.

Split off: *generate signature* and *update signature* are CMD-428; the quick fix that adds a registry (Q4) is CMD-420's, with the known packs from registries.

Part 3 of the browser preview (CMD-30; [editor support](docs/editor-support.md), decision 8; [the language server](docs/language-server.md), decision 13), in `crates/lsp/src/preview/`:

- `tiny_http` (named in the approved design) on `127.0.0.1`, on ports chosen at start: the page on one, the frames on another, so the frames have an origin of their own;
- a random token in the page's path, and a `Host` check that refuses any request whose `Host` isn't the server's own;
- the page (`crates/lsp/preview/page/`) and its bundle (`crates/lsp/preview/compmd/`, the frames and the manifest), which `npm-packages/devtools/preview/` builds with `@compmd/vite` (CMD-425), served by `compmd-host`'s routes and headers, with the trust the document's configuration gives; the bundle is built without a `source`, so the page takes the frames' origin from the server (`globalThis.__COMPMD__`, as `tauri-plugin-compmd` gives it);
- the routes the page reads beside it, under its token (CMD-425, Q1 b):
  - `events`: server-sent events, `change` with the JSON `{ "text", "line" }` for each change of the document (the line from 1), and `reload` when what it reads changed (a file it imports, its configuration, its packs);
  - `request.json`: the document's request but its text: its path, the files it reads (as the server's processor read them, under its root only), its policy, ambient packs and allowed origins;
  - `registry/`: npm's protocol for every pack it imports: the project's own (folder and installed packs, as tarballs with their integrity) and those its configuration's registries give, as the fetch thread has them;
- the bundle embedded by the crate's `build.rs`, as `tauri-plugin-compmd`'s `build::bundle` embeds an app's: without a built bundle, a warning, and the preview says it isn't in this build.

## Tests

The server's routes, the token, the `Host` check, the events, `request.json` and `registry/`, over HTTP on `127.0.0.1`.
